    <tr><td>Timelines</td><td>Reference to be entered upon within three weeks; Section 12(2) disclosures within three weeks thereafter</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Institutional framework</td><td>Arbitration under the rules of the Delhi International Arbitration Centre; fees per the DIAC Schedule of Fees</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Merits</td><td>All pleas kept open; the Court's observations confined to the disposal of the petition</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Registry</td><td>Directed to communicate the order to the arbitrator, including by email</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Why parties choose this route</h2>
<p>A Section 34 challenge fought to judgment carries risk on both sides: the award-holder may lose everything if the court finds patent illegality, and the challenger may face execution with accumulated interest if the challenge fails. Where a defect in the award is tolerably clear to both camps — a misreading of the contract, a gap in reasons, an issue decided without evidence — consenting to a set-aside with an immediate fresh reference converts years of uncertain litigation into a bounded second arbitration. For public bodies, it also avoids a reasoned judicial finding of illegality against the award they obtained or resisted.</p>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Contested Section 34</h3>
    <p>Limited grounds, no re-appreciation of evidence, outcome binary — the award survives or falls. Appeals under Section 37 can add years. The court cannot modify the award or substitute its own view on merits.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Consent disposal</h3>
    <p>The award goes by agreement; the claim returns to a tribunal both sides accept; timelines, institution and fees are fixed in the same order; every merits plea survives for the new arbitrator.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The institutional details are not boilerplate</h2>
<p>Three features of the order repay attention. The direction to make Section 12(2) disclosures within a fixed window ensures the new tribunal's independence is tested at the threshold, not discovered mid-reference. Anchoring the arbitration to the DIAC rules gives the fresh proceeding a procedural spine — timelines, fee certainty under the DIAC schedule, and administrative support — which is particularly valuable where the first arbitration has already consumed years. And the express statement that observations in the judgment will not influence the arbitrator protects the fresh adjudication from being colonised by remarks made in a consent context.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For parties weighing a consent set-aside:</strong> confirm what exactly is being set aside (the whole award or a severable part); insist that the order record that all pleas remain open; fix the appointment, disclosure timelines and fee framework in the same order; and consider limitation — a claim restored to arbitration should move immediately, not rest on the order.</p>
</div>

<h2>The larger point</h2>
<p>Section 34 is usually described in terms of its narrow grounds. This disposal is a reminder that the provision also operates inside a party-driven system: where both sides accept that an award cannot stand, the court's role is to give that agreement an orderly legal effect — ending the flawed award, constituting the new tribunal, and keeping the merits untouched. This article is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
